\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The vacuum Markov kernel}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Use the full transfer kernel #{K_T}, product Haar probability
 #{\nu} and everywhere positive representative #{\Omega} from
 \ref{fdip-00N6}. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
\pi(dU)&=\Omega(U)^2\nu(dU),\\
Q(U,dU')&=\frac{K_T(U,U')\Omega(U')}
 {\lambda_0\Omega(U)}\,\nu(dU')
\end{aligned}}
defines a Borel probability transition kernel reversible for
 #{\pi}. Its operator on #{L^2(\pi)} is conjugate to the
 normalized full transfer by the unitary
##{W:L^2(\pi)\longrightarrow L^2(\nu),\qquad Wf=\Omega f,
 \qquad WQW^{-1}=T/\lambda_0.}
The stationary discrete-time Markov chain with initial law
 #{\pi} and transition #{Q} realizes the vacuum correlations
 of bounded gauge-invariant spatial multiplication observables,
 at integer multiples of the chosen time step #{a_t}.}

\proof{
\p{The positive Borel density is defined at every #{U}.
 Integrating its row gives one by the pointwise eigen-equation
 of \ref{fdip-00N6}. Its dependence on #{U} is measurable
 by integration of a bounded Borel function; the positive bounds
 on #{\Omega} suffice here. Symmetry of the real kernel gives
##{\pi(dU)Q(U,dU')=
 \lambda_0^{-1}\Omega(U)K_T(U,U')\Omega(U')
 \,\nu(dU)\nu(dU'),}
which is invariant under exchanging #{U,U'}. This proves
 detailed balance and stationarity.}

\p{The identity #{\|Wf\|_{L^2(\nu)}^2=\|f\|_{L^2(\pi)}^2}
 proves isometry; #{u\mapsto u/\Omega} is its inverse
 because its #{L^2(\pi)} norm equals #{\|u\|_{L^2(\nu)}}.
 Direct substitution of the kernel proves the conjugacy on
 bounded functions, then on #{L^2} by density and boundedness.
 The chain's finite-dimensional laws are the iterated integrals
 #{\pi(dU_0)Q(U_0,dU_1)\cdots Q(U_{m-1},dU_m)}.
 Their consistency gives the law on #{X^{\mathbb N}}.
 For bounded functions and integers #{0=n_0\le n_1\le\cdots\le n_m},
 the resulting expectation is
##{\mathbb E_\pi\prod_{j=0}^m f_j(U_{n_j})
 =\langle\Omega,M_{f_0}(T/\lambda_0)^{n_1-n_0}
 M_{f_1}\cdots(T/\lambda_0)^{n_m-n_{m-1}}M_{f_m}\Omega\rangle.}
This follows by inserting #{W^{-1}W} between the transition
 powers and multipliers. For gauge-invariant #{f_j}, all
 factors preserve the physical subspace, so its two centered
 insertions give \ref{fdip-00N7}.}
}

\p{The construction of a path law from a measurable probability
 transition kernel is formalized by
 \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.markovChainLaw}, with the
 finite-prefix identity
 \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.markovChainLaw_map_prefix_succ_eq_compProd}.
 These declarations supply the discrete path-law step; the
 vacuum density, normalization and reversibility were proved above.}
